// - gzip framing, writing: ID1 ID2, CM 8, FLG with FTEXT, FEXTRA, FNAME and
//   FCOMMENT as the header says, MTIME (0 for none), XFL (4 at levels 0 and 1,
//   2 at level 9, else 0), OS (255, unknown, unless the header says), the extra field (at most
//   65535 bytes), the name and comment cut at a zero byte (they are ISO 8859-1
//   bytes, zero-terminated); never FHCRC (the header CRC-16). One member per
//   stream. Reading: all of those fields are kept in a GzipHeader; FHCRC is
//   checked when present; reserved flag bits, a method other than 8 and a bad
//   magic are BadHeader; the CRC-32 and ISIZE (the size modulo 2^32) of every
//   member are verified. Members after the first are read and their data joined;
//   zero bytes between members and after the last are padding and ignored (as
//   GNU gzip and Python's gzip module do); any other byte after a member that is
//   not the start of another is TrailingData. A name or comment over 1 MiB is
//   refused. The header (name, comment, extra) is read whole or not at all, so a
//   header cut by the input is waited for.
// - No global and no thread-local. The constant tables of RFC 1951 (lengths,
//   distances, code-length order: the four 'imm' arrays and one const in
//   tables.cone) are moved here from imagedecode unchanged, not added; the CRC
//   table and the Huffman tables are values inside the values that use them.
//   No new module-level table or const was added while the question of
//   whether one is allowed is open: the fixed Huffman codes are built at run time
//   into each Inflater and Deflater's own state, the hash-chain heads are Lists
//   in the Deflater, and even the scalar limits (tokens in a block, look-ahead,
//   the default output cap) are one-line functions, not consts. This costs no
//   measurable speed (a call that returns a literal is inlined); the only
//   module-level names in the package are the five RFC 1951 tables and
